WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
there was a moment if I was going  unconscious and there was just sort of  lack of concern about the life that they  had left laying in that alley  my brother was shot by an  african-american when I was a young kid  and so I had that fear of  african-americans fear of anybody pretty  much who wasn't like me and the anger  during my adolescent years into my 20s  it was just over the top I just was one  of those kids that never felt there was  ever any issue or a problem with who I  was it wasn't until I was 13 and sort of  made that clear and the reaction of my  family was I was thrown out for me you  know a lot of times to escape the  suburbs we were being picked on by the  jocks or the football players or the  popular kids or whatever we you know in  the late 70s early 1980s punk rockers  were freaks we would go into Hollywood  and we were on the rampage the police  didn't like us nobody liked us I liked  that negative attention oh these people  are weird these people are scary after  concerts we would hang out at this hot  dog stand called okie dogs there were  little pockets of individual groups  within the larger tribe who were more  violent than others when my mom threw me  out because of the fact that I was gay I  figured well show her I'm gonna go to  Hollywood become a successful actor and  then I'll just be like okay whatever I  ended up you know living in a park  directly across the street from the very  same hamburger stand that Tim and a lot  of his crew hung out at as well except  that I had never seen them there until  that one night that I was there hanging  out like I normally do playing video  games so as I looked up and saw them  sort of kind of coming across the street  being very loud and boisterous there was  absolutely no concern it wasn't until  whether it was Tim or someone else  yelled let's kill the [ __ ] and what  ended up happening is all of that energy  from all of however many there were was  descended upon me  in what was a very brutal attack in  Matthew was being kicked on the ground  and he was still moving around and there  was like several people I came up to  them Esav what's you know come on do  what you're supposed to do what was  taking so long  and I actually kicked Matthew in the  head and there was high-fives and then  it was like but there was a girl with us  was screaming you guys are so messed up  and then we said oh wow you know maybe  we killed this guy so we got in our cars  and we left and when I come to nobody  was there everybody had left Amber's  town nobody was there putting their hand  on my shoulder nobody was saying I'm  calling the police nobody was telling me  was gonna be okay nobody was like I take  you to the hospital throwing me in the  car there's a moment of this this  violent attack based in hatred and fear  and then absolutely being invisible  there was a murder that took place in  this country that to me like ripped a  hole in the soul of this country and  brought a nation together  the connection I have with him is that I  survived and he didn't and I remember  clients saying to me if you're so  bothered by it what have you done about  it  I said well nothing I don't know what I  can do and she goes well then stop  complaining to me about it you can  either sit back and be complacent and  accept what you see in the news or you  can take part and be something of that  as an instrument of change and I ended  up believing about leaving that career  and moving on to the Museum of Tolerance  in Los Angeles just to volunteer and  that become the manager of that Museum I  actually ended up getting involved with  a rather large organized national hate  group moved up the ranks and then I met  a young lady and had a child and things  started to change eventually I did make  it out of the white racialist movement I  said that I was moving out of Southern  California I was gonna get out of this  cesspool Los Angeles and raised my  family in a whiter brighter world and  was no longer compatible with my son's  mother I was watching my son being  influenced by the politics and by the  propaganda and I ended up marrying a  Jewish woman moved back to Southern  California and went to go see TJ Lydon  speak at a at a church and he said that  he was leaving the Museum of Tolerance  to go do his own thing and I asked him  do you think  they would let me take over you gave me  a card I called the museum and I've been  there for over a decade as a result  that's where I met Matthew a teacher  call she had a group of students that  she was bringing and they wanted a  powerful speaker to address I invited to  him or asked him to go to have coffee so  I could ask him get his opinion because  the issue happened to be dealing with  white supremacy in this specific  community and at this school so what  ended up happening is his Tim was  talking to me completely off topic I was  like you know what part of Southern  California who grew up in your hat  you know I've never asked you that and  the subject came up about a HAMP the  hamburger stand okie dogs it comes out  that we both realized almost within  seconds of each other who the other one  was talking to there was a surge of  emotion but not the ones you would think  it wasn't anger it wasn't like I wanted  to throw something at him there was just  a surge of emotions of there was almost  a fear I was scared I was nervous and  the only thing I knew how to do was to  leave my best thinking was all right I'm  going to try to have conversations with  Tim really the answer I wanted was why I  went over and over and over and over in  my mind trying to minimize my part  trying to blame Matthew trying to blame  you know the people I was with somebody  said to do it or you know just trying to  come up with an excuse and at the end of  the day there is no excuse you know I  wasn't planning on apologizing at that  moment it just sort of came out we were  asked by the museum to start doing  presentations together my first thought  was  no I haven't processed this enough to  sit on a stage and for 300 people there  was still a part of me that saw the Tim  I knew the eight months before realizing  who he was and yes he was this  incredible person who had changed his  life and I've always admired people and  were able to do that especially if  you're in so deep and then you take the  courage in the stand it's a level of  honesty and willingness to share and  spend time with me was a huge part of me  being able to let go not forget and be  able to see him as if as a person in  human being and then being able to  forgive him completely entirely it never  was a friendship of let's go to the  movies let's have dinner let's hang out  with each other's families  it was more of we shared a common bond  and it's difficult as it can be at times  the common goal is to share this story  in order to reach out and reach people  that change is possible not only has  change possible but forgiveness as  possible healing is possible the world  but especially this nation it has this  this wound that has never been healed  and recent activities that have happened  is like tearing that wound open and  sticking your finger in it and until  that wound can be truly healed through  knowing one another really really  knowing one another not oh I have a  black friend or I have a white friend do  you know them do you know their middle  name do you know their parents have you  ever been in their house I do believe  that it is progressing forward and it is  becoming better I just think it's taking  much longer than it needs to if Matthew  and I can heal  through forgiveness I think other people  can as well

